# agent-fleet — Caddy reverse proxy + automatic TLS (P3-10 stage 2)
#
# Just point the public domain {$PUBLIC_DOMAIN} at this host and Caddy obtains and
# renews a Let's Encrypt certificate via ACME automatically (each company only needs
# a domain and a DNS A/AAAA record). Caddy also passes WebSocket Upgrade through, so
# terminal ws:// traffic works as-is.
#
# Network assumption (docs/log/p3-10-packaging.md §20.4): CP and Caddy are BOTH
# host-net. The CP binds 127.0.0.1:8099 (loopback only) and is never exposed
# directly; Caddy shares the same host loopback and reaches 127.0.0.1:8099. A
# bridge-net Caddy could not reach the host-net CP's loopback bind — the CP would
# have to bind 0.0.0.0, breaking the loopback invariant — hence rejected.

{$PUBLIC_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8099
}

# --- Alternative 1: no public domain / internal testing (self-signed, Caddy local CA)
# Comment out the block above and enable this instead (browsers show a CA warning):
#
#   {$PUBLIC_DOMAIN} {
#   	tls internal
#   	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8099
#   }
#
# --- Alternative 2: fronting with your own existing proxy / load balancer ---------
# Drop this caddy service from compose and route from your proxy to the CP. The CP
# keeps its loopback bind, so either run your proxy on the same host (targeting
# 127.0.0.1:8099) or change CP_ADDR to an internal-network interface (protect it
# with a firewall / security group so it is not exposed). Either way, pass
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://<public domain> to the CP (source of the redirect_uri).
